```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
The fault is a missing BGP network origination statement on leaf_router_0_0
(AS 65200). Its FRR running configuration contains neighbor definitions for
both upstream spines (172.16.0.4 / spine_router_0_0 and 172.16.0.8 /
spine_router_0_1) but has no `address-family ipv4 unicast` block and
therefore no `network 10.0.0.0/24` statement. Without this, FRR never
installs the connected prefix into the BGP Loc-RIB and never generates an
UPDATE advertisement to either spine. This was confirmed by spine_router_0_0
reporting "0 accepted prefixes" from neighbor 172.16.0.5 and by the
complete absence of 10.0.0.0/24 in both spines' and super_spine_router_0's
RIBs. leaf_router_0_1's config provided the direct comparison: it carries the
identical neighbor setup PLUS the correct `network 10.0.1.0/24` under
address-family ipv4 unicast, which is why 10.0.1.0/24 propagates fabric-wide
while 10.0.0.0/24 does not. All BGP sessions remain Established because the
session establishment is independent of prefix advertisement — the peering
handshake succeeds but leaf_router_0_0 simply sends no prefixes. The root
cause is a missing `network 10.0.0.0/24` statement in leaf_router_0_0's BGP
address-family ipv4 unicast configuration. The fix is to add the following to
leaf_router_0_0's FRR config:

  router bgp 65200
   address-family ipv4 unicast
    network 10.0.0.0/24
   exit-address-family

This will cause leaf_router_0_0 to originate and advertise the prefix to both
spines, which will relay it to super_spine_router_0 and leaf_router_0_1,
restoring end-to-end reachability between pc_0_0 and pc_0_1.
=== END ===
```